    "content": "Title: [2302.07960] Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\nURL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07960\nPublished: 2023-02-15T04:11:12.000Z\nAuthor: Fatemi, Bahare, Duval, Quentin, Girdhar, Rohit, Drozdzal, Michal, Romero-Soriano, Adriana\nHighlights:\nallergens, and ease culinary exploration in everyone's kitchen. To address ingredient substitution, we build a benchmark, composed of a dataset of substitution pairs with standardized splits, evaluation metrics, and baselines. We further introduce\n\n---\n\nTitle: Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\nURL: https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2302.07960\nPublished: 2023-02-15T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Bahare Fatemi, Quentin Duval, Rohit Girdhar, Michal Drozdzal, Adriana Romero\nHighlights:\n## GISMo Implementation Details\n\nWe implemented our model in PyTorch [44], used deep graph library (DGL) [57] for the sparse operations, and used Adam [30] as the optimizer. We performed early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the vali dation set for all models, and used a maximum number of epochs of 1000. We tuned the learning rate lr using the set { 0.005, 0.001, 0.0005, 0.0001, 0.00005, 0.00001, 0.000005} , and both the number of features F and ingredient embed ding dimension d using the set { 200, 300, 400, 500, 600} . Our final model consists of an ingredient encoder (IE) composed of two GIN [59] layers, and an ingredient substi tution decoder (ISD) composed of a multi-layer perceptron with three layers. The model uses F = 300. The input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredi ent embedding dimension is set to d = 300. The model is trained with a learning rate lr = 0.00005, and is regularized using a weight decay of 0.0001 and a dropout rate of 0.25.\n\n---\n\nTitle: [2302.07960] Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\nURL: https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2302.07960\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Appendix A GISMo Implementation Details\n\nWe implemented our model in PyTorch [44], used deep graph library (DGL) [57] for the sparse operations, and used Adam [30] as the optimizer. We performed early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the validation set for all models, and used a maximum number of epochs of $1000$ . We tuned the learning rate $lr$ using the set $\\{0.005,0.001,0.0005,0.0001,0.00005,0.00001,0.000005\\}$ , and both the number of features $F$ and ingredient embedding dimension $d$ using the set $\\{200,300,400,500,600\\}$ .\n\nOur final model consists of an ingredient encoder ( $\\mathrm{IE}$ ) composed of two GIN [59] layers, and an ingredient substitution decoder ( $\\mathrm{ISD}$ ) composed of a multi-layer perceptron with three layers. The model uses $F=300$ . The input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredient embedding dimension is set to $d=300$ . The model is trained with a learning rate $lr=0.00005$ , and is regularized using a weight decay of $0.0001$ and a dropout rate of $0.25$ .\n\n---\n\nTitle: N/A\nURL: https://export.arxiv.org/pdf/2302.07960v1.pdf\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n#### A. GISMo Implementation Details\n\nWe implemented our model in PyTorch [44], used deep graph library (DGL) [57] for the sparse operations, and used Adam [30] as the optimizer. We performed early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the vali dation set for all models, and used a maximum number of epochs of 1000. We tuned the learning rate lr using the set { 0.005, 0.001, 0.0005, 0.0001, 0.00005, 0.00001, 0.000005} , and both the number of features F and ingredient embed ding dimension d using the set { 200, 300, 400, 500, 600} . Our final model consists of an ingredient encoder (IE) composed of two GIN [59] layers, and an ingredient substi tution decoder (ISD) composed of a multi-layer perceptron with three layers. The model uses F = 300. The input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredi ent embedding dimension is set to d = 300. The model is trained with a learning rate lr = 0.00005, and is regularized using a weight decay of 0.0001 and a dropout rate of 0.25.\n\n---\n\nTitle: N/A\nURL: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2302.07960\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nMartin-Bautista. A word embedding-based method for un\u0002supervised adaptation of cooking recipes. IEEE Access,\n9:27389–27404, 2021. 1, 8\n[40] Taichi Nishimura, Atsushi Hashimoto, and Shinsuke Mori.\nProcedural text generation from a photo sequence. In Pro\u0002ceedings of the 12th International Conference on Natural\nLanguage Generation, pages 409–414, Tokyo, Japan, Oct.–\nNov. 2019. Association for Computational Linguistics. 8\n[41] Siyuan Pan, Ling Dai, Xuhong Hou, Huating Li, and Bin\nSheng. Chefgan: Food image generation from recipes. In\nProceedings of the 28th ACM International Conference on\nMultimedia, MM ’20, page 4244–4252, New York, NY,\nUSA, 2020. Association for Computing Machinery. 8\n[42] Yuran Pan, Qiangwen Xu, and Yanjun Li. Food recipe alter\u0002nation and generation with natural language processing tech\u0002niques. In 2020 IEEE 36th International Conference on Data\nEngineering Workshops (ICDEW), pages 94–97, 2020. 8\n[43] Donghyeon Park, Keonwoo Kim, Seoyoon Kim, Michael\nSpranger, and Jaewoo Kang. Flavorgraph: a large-scale\nfood-chemical graph for generating food representations and\nrecommending food pairings. Scientific reports, 11(1):1–13,\n2021. 1, 4, 5, 6, 8\n[44] Adam Paszke, Sam Gross, Soumith Chintala, Gregory\nChanan, Edward Yang, Zachary DeVito, Zeming Lin, Al\u0002ban Desmaison, Luca Antiga, and Adam Lerer. Automatic\ndifferentiation in pytorch. 2017. 4, 11\n[45] Chantal Pellegrini, Ege Ozsoy, Monika Wintergerst, and ¨\nGeorg Groh. Exploiting food embeddings for ingredient sub\u0002stitution. In HEALTHINF, pages 67–77, 2021. 1, 2, 5, 6, 8\n[46] Luis Pineda, Amaia Salvador, Michal Drozdzal, and Adriana\nRomero. Elucidating image-to-set prediction: An analysis of\nmodels, losses and datasets. CoRR, abs/1904.05709, 2019. 4\n[47] Alec Radford, Jong Wook Kim, Chris Hallacy, Aditya\nRamesh, Gabriel Goh, Sandhini Agarwal, Girish Sastry,\nAmanda Askell, Pamela Mishkin, Jack Clark, et al. Learn\u0002ing transferable visual models from natural language super\u0002vision. arXiv preprint arXiv:2103.00020, 2021. 3\n[48] Alec Radford, Jeff Wu, Rewon Child, David Luan, Dario\nAmodei, and Ilya Sutskever. Language models are unsuper\u0002vised multitask learners. 2019. 7\n[49] Amaia Salvador, Michal Drozdzal, Xavier Giro-i Nieto, and\nAdriana Romero. Inverse cooking: Recipe generation from\nfood images. In CVPR, pages 10453–10462, 2019. 2, 4, 7, 8\n[50] Amaia Salvador, Erhan Gundogdu, Loris Bazzani, and\nMichael Donoser. Revamping cross-modal recipe retrieval\nwith hierarchical transformers and self-supervised learning.\nIn The IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern\nRecognition (CVPR), June 2021. 8\n[51] Amaia Salvador, Nicholas Hynes, Yusuf Aytar, Javier Marin,\nFerda Ofli, Ingmar Weber, and Antonio Torralba. Learning\ncross-modal embeddings for cooking recipes and food im\u0002ages. In CVPR, pages 3020–3028, 2017. 2, 4\n[52] Thibault Sellam, Dipanjan Das, and Ankur P. Parikh.\nBLEURT: learning robust metrics for text generation. CoRR,\nabs/2004.04696, 2020. 7\n[53] Yuka Shidochi, Tomokazu Takahashi, Ichiro Ide, and Hiroshi\nMurase. Finding replaceable materials in cooking recipe\ntexts considering characteristic cooking actions. In Proceed\u0002ings of the ACM multimedia 2009 workshop on Multimedia\nfor cooking and eating activities, pages 9–14, 2009. 1, 8\n[54] Sola S Shirai, Oshani Seneviratne, Minor E Gordon, Ching\u0002Hua Chen, and Deborah L McGuinness. Identifying ingredi\u0002ent substitutions using a knowledge graph of food. Frontiers\nin Artificial Intelligence, 3, 2020. 1, 8\n10\n[55] Sushant Singh and Ausif Mahmood. The NLP cookbook:\nModern recipes for transformer based deep learning archi\u0002tectures. CoRR, abs/2104.10640, 2021. 8\n[56] Hao Wang, Guosheng Lin, Steven C. H. Hoi, and Chunyan\nMiao. Structure-aware generation network for recipe genera\u0002tion from images. In Andrea Vedaldi, Horst Bischof, Thomas\nBrox, and Jan-Michael Frahm, editors, Computer Vision –\nECCV 2020, pages 359–374, Cham, 2020. Springer Interna\u0002tional Publishing. 8\n[57] Minjie Wang, Lingfan Yu, Da Zheng, Quan Gan, Yu Gai,\nZihao Ye, Mufei Li, Jinjing Zhou, Qi Huang, Chao Ma,\net al. Deep graph library: Towards efficient and scalable\ndeep learning on graphs. arXiv preprint arXiv:1909.01315,\n2019. 4, 11\n[58] Shanchan Wu and Yifan He. Enriching pre-trained language\nmodel with entity information for relation classification. In\nProceedings of the 28th ACM international conference on\ninformation and knowledge management, pages 2361–2364,\n2019. 1, 8\n[59] Keyulu Xu, Weihua Hu, Jure Leskovec, and Stefanie Jegelka.\nHow powerful are graph neural networks? arXiv preprint\narXiv:1810.00826, 2018. 3, 11\n[60] Ryosuke Yamanishi, Naoki Shino, Yoko Nishihara, Junichi\nFukumoto, and Aya Kaizaki. Alternative-ingredient rec\u0002ommendation based on co-occurrence relation on recipe\ndatabase. Procedia Computer Science, 60:986–993, 2015.\n1, 8\n[61] Rex Ying, Ruining He, Kaifeng Chen, Pong Eksombatchai,\nWilliam L Hamilton, and Jure Leskovec. Graph convo\u0002lutional neural networks for web-scale recommender sys\u0002tems. In Proceedings of the 24th ACM SIGKDD Interna\u0002tional Conference on Knowledge Discovery & Data Mining,\npages 974–983, 2018. 3, 5\n[62] Tianyi Zhang*, Varsha Kishore*, Felix Wu*, Kilian Q.\nWeinberger, and Yoav Artzi. Bertscore: Evaluating text gen\u0002eration with bert. In International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations, 2020. 7\n[63] Bin Zhu and Chong-Wah Ngo. Cookgan: Causality based\ntext-to-image synthesis. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF\nConference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\n(CVPR), June 2020. 8\n[64] Bin Zhu, Chong-Wah Ngo, Jingjing Chen, and Yanbin Hao.\nR2gan: Cross-modal recipe retrieval with generative adver\u0002sarial network. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference\non Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), June\n2019. 8\nA. GISMo Implementation Details\nWe implemented our model in PyTorch [44], used deep\ngraph library (DGL) [57] for the sparse operations, and used\nAdam [30] as the optimizer. We performed early stopping\nand hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the vali\u0002dation set for all models, and used a maximum number of\nepochs of 1000. We tuned the learning rate lr using the set\n{0.005, 0.001, 0.0005, 0.0001, 0.00005, 0.00001, 0.000005},\nand both the number of features F and ingredient embed\u0002ding dimension d using the set {200, 300, 400, 500, 600}.\nOur final model consists of an ingredient encoder (IE)\ncomposed of two GIN [59] layers, and an ingredient substi\u0002tution decoder (ISD) composed of a multi-layer perceptron\nwith three layers. The model uses F = 300. The input\ningredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredi\u0002ent embedding dimension is set to d = 300. The model is\ntrained with a learning rate lr = 0.00005, and is regularized\nusing a weight decay of 0.0001 and a dropout rate of 0.25.\nB. Visual inspection of recipes\nAll ingredient predictions and recipe generations visual\u0002ized in the figures are based on our best ViT-16 MLS.\nWe first look at the ability of GISMo to provide inter\u0002esting and plausible substitutions. Figure 5 shows substitu\u0002tion examples when using ground truth ingredients as input,\nand Figure 6 depicts the predicted substitutions when using\ningredients predicted by the personalized inverse cooking\npipeline as input. In both cases, GISMo comes up with\nreasonable substitutions given the recipe context. We also\ncompare the suggestions of GISMo to those of the LT-Freq\n(Lookup Table + Frequency) approach, our strongest base\u0002line, in Figures 7 – 8, showing qualitatively that GISMo can\nadapt to context and to propose substitutions not seen dur\u0002ing training.\nWe then select some of the top-5 substitutions proposed\nby GISMo in Figure 6 to generate new recipes with the\npersonalized inverse cooking pipeline, and experiment\nwith the effect of replacing two ingredients with GISMo’s\nsuggestions instead of just one. The results are shown in\nFigure 9. We extend this qualitative experiment to highlight\nthe importance of using reliable candidate substitutions\nfrom GISMo, as opposed to random substitutions. Figure\n10 presents these results. Note that random substitutions\nare often ignored by the recipe generator, leading to lower\nIoU (consistency) scores.\nFinally, we look at some failure modes of the person\u0002alized inverse cooking pipeline when used in combination\nwith GISMo, pointing to potential areas of improvement.\nFigure 11 shows how the creativity of GISMo to come\nup with ingredient substitutions might be negatively im\u0002pacted by incomplete, wrong, or near duplicate ingredient\npredictions of the personalized inverse cooking pipeline.\nRecall that, in this case, the ingredients predicted by the\ninverse cooking pipeline constitute the context of GISMo,\ntherefore impacting the kind of substitutions that GISMo\ncan propose. Improving the ingredient prediction step in the\npersonalized inverse cooking pipeline holds the promise to\nimprove GISMo’s suggestions as a result. Moreover, Figure\n12 shows the phenomenon of recipe collapse that some\u0002times occurs when performing (even minor) substitutions\nwith the personalized inverse cooking pipeline. However,\n11\nas shown in Figure 13, this phenomenon also appears in\nthe inverse cooking pipeline which does not consider any\nsubstitution, pointing to potential areas of improvement\nof the recipe generator module of the personalized inverse\ncooking pipeline.\n12\nReal Ingredients:\n- brine\n- chicken_piece\n- kaffir_lime_leaf\n- light_brown_sugar\n- light_coconut_milk\n- marinade\n- red_curry_paste\nGenerated recipe: Thai chicken\n- preheat oven to 350 degrees f.\n- place chicken in a large bowl and pour in the coconut milk, \ncurry paste, brown sugar, lime juice, and red pepper paste.\n- stir to combine.\n- pour marinade over chicken and stir to coat.\n- place chicken in a 9x13 baking dish and bake for 1 hour.\n- remove chicken from oven and let cool for 10 minutes.\n- slice chicken into thin strips and place on top of chicken.\nchicken_piece:\n- chicken_breast\n- chicken\n- fried_tofu\n- turkey\n- tofu\nred_curry_paste:\n- curry_powder\n- cayenne_pepper\n- pepper\n- sugar\n- saffron\nkaffir_lime_leaf:\n- lemon_juice\n- ground_ginger\n- orange_juice\n- fresh_ginger\n- lemon_zest\nlight_brown_sugar:\n- honey\n- maple_syrup\n- white_sugar\n- sucanat\n- dark_brown_sugar\nFigure 5. Top 5 suggestions of GISMo for 4 different ingredients on a Thai chicken recipe. Here, GISMo is used on the ground truth\ningredients of a recipe. GISMo’s suggestions to replace chicken include vegetarian options although the chicken piece ingredient itself\nnever appears together with tofu in the training set.\nPred Ingredients:\n- all_purpose_flour\n- baking_powder\n- baking_soda\n- banana\n- butter\n- egg\n- flour\n- salt\n- sugar\n- vanilla\n- walnut\nGenerated recipe: Banana nut bread\n- preheat oven to 350 degrees.\n- grease and flour a 9 x 5 inch loaf pan.\n- in a large bowl, cream together the butter and sugar.\n- beat in the eggs one at a time.\n- beat in the bananas and vanilla.\n- in a separate bowl, combine the flour, baking powder, baking soda and salt.\n- add the flour mixture to the creamed mixture alternately with the milk.\n- fold in the walnuts.\n- pour the batter into the prepared pan.\n- bake for 1 hour or until a toothpick inserted in the center comes out clean.\n- cool in the pan for 10 minutes before removing to a wire rack to cool completely.\nwalnut:\n- pecan\n- chocolat_chip\n- almond\n- mini_chocolat_chip\n- raisin\nbanana:\n- applesauce\n- apple\n- pumpkin\n- buttermilk\n- blueberry\nvanilla:\n- almond_extract\n- lemon_extract\n- banana_extract\n- orange_extract\n- maple_extract\nsalt:\n- garlic_salt\n- sea_salt\n- hot_coffee\n- banana\n- chocolat_chip\nFigure 6. Top 5 suggestions of GISMo for 4 different ingredient substitutions. In this case, GISMo takes the predicted ingredients of the\ninverse cooking pipeline as input. GISMo’s replacement suggestions for walnuts include other types of nuts as well as chocolate chips and\nraisin, which could be good replacements in this context.\n13\nTitle: Beer bread\nIngredients:\n- all_purpose_flour\n-"
