d sources (s1). Values → against page.\n\n\"6,653\" is both a factual claim component AND a value. The value 6,653 against the page → 6,653 ✓.\n\nThis is genuinely ambiguous. I'll go with UNSUPPORTED and explain clearly, because the cited source s1 (results row) clearly doesn't support the rationale, and the instruction leans toward \"factual claim checked against cited sources\".\n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider whether \"PARTIAL\" is more appropriate. PARTIAL = \"some parts supported, some not\". Here:\n- The prediction parts (a,d) need no source (not \"supported\" but \"exempt\").\n- The factual parts (b,c) are unsupported by the citation.\n\nIf I treat \"needs no source\" as \"satisfied\", then (a,d) are satisfied, (b,c) are unsupported → PARTIAL.\n\nIf I treat the item as: does the cited source support the item? The cited s1 doesn't support (b,c) → UNSUPPORTED (the citation fails to support the factual claims).\n\nI think PARTIAL is more informative here: the prediction is fine, but the factual justification is unsupported by the citation. But UNSUPPORTED is also defensible.\n\nI'll go with UNSUPPORTED, because the core factual justification (the \"because\" clause) is not supported by the cited source, and that's the part requiring support. Actually, you know what, let me reconsider the whole hypothesis framing.\n\nActually, I think the most accurate is: the hypothesis's factual claims (b, c) ARE on the page (I verified 6,653 and small GNN via the fetched page). The citation points to s1 (wrong quote), but the facts are verifiable on the same page. \n\nGiven \"Decide only from the pages you fetch\", and the facts ARE on the fetched page, one could argue SUPPORTED (facts on page) but with a note that the cited s1 is the wrong quote.\n\nBut the instruction is explicit: \"check each factual claim in it against those sources\". \"Those sources\" = the ones the item cites = [s1]. s1 doesn't support. → UNSUPPORTED.\n\nI'll go UNSUPPORTED. Final.\n\nHmm, wait,