= 600 ✓.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me step back and think about the overall intent.\n\nI think the intent is: for each card item, verify the factual claims are supported by the cited sources (or the page). The key is whether the item's factual content is ACCURATE per the pages, and whether the citations are adequate.\n\nFor closest_prior: the factual content is accurate (tuned 200-600 ✓, kept 300 ✓, no width trend ✓, max 600 ✓). The citations: s6 supports \"tuned 200-600\" and \"max 600\"; s1 is the results row (supports \"GISMo final model results\"). The \"kept 300\" and \"no width trend\" are supported by the page (appendix and absence of trend), even if not perfectly quoted.\n\nI think closest_prior is SUPPORTED overall (the page supports all its factual claims), even though the \"kept 300\" is in the appendix just after the s6 quote.\n\nFor hypothesis: the factual claims (6,653, small GNN) are on the page (vocab section, appendix). So they're \"against the page\" supported. But the cited s1 (results row) doesn't support them. The difference from closest_prior: in closest_prior, s6 (one of the cited sources) DOES support the width tuning (the main claim). In hypothesis, s1 (the ONLY cited source) supports NOTHING in the hypothesis (it's just a results number, while the hypothesis claims things about vocab size and GNN size).\n\nSo:\n- closest_prior: main claim (width tuning 200-600) supported by cited s6 → SUPPORTED (with minor note).\n- hypothesis: main factual claim (ranks 6,653 with small GNN) NOT supported by cited s1 → UNSUPPORTED.\n\nThis feels right. The difference is whether the cited source actually supports the core factual claim.\n\nOK, I'm confident now. Final decisions:\n\n1. card:question → PARTIAL (20.56 on page; 34.53 unsourced).\n2. card:hypothesis → UNSUPPORTED (cited s1 = results row; doesn't support 6,653/small-GNN rationale).\n3. card:if_lever → SUPPORTED (prediction).\n4. card:if_not → SUPPORTED (prediction).\n5. card:test_summary → PARTIAL (20.56 on page; 34.53 unsourced).\n6. card:closest_prior → SUPPORTED.\n7. card:left_open → UNSUPPORTED (novelty claim, no source).\n8. card:anchor → SUPPORTED.\n9. card:limits → UNSUPPORTED (sustainability-labeling claim, no source).\n10. src:s1 → SUPPORTED.\n11. src:s11 → SUPPORTED.\n12. src:s6 → SUPPORTED.\n13. num:vocab_size → SUPPORTED (6653).\n14. num:n_test → SUPPORTED (10747).\n15. num:n_seeds → SUPPORTED (5).\n\nWait, let me reconsider card:question once more. Should it be UNSUPPORTED rather than PARTIAL? \n\nThe question is fundamentally a question (research question). The \"20.56 vs 34.53 gap\" is the context. 20.56 is verifiable. 34.53 is not. Since the question embeds a factual premise that's only half-verifiable, PARTIAL seems right. But one could argue the question's premise (that there's a \"gap\" of 20.56 vs 34.53 between \"two GISMo protocols\") is a single factual claim, and since 34.53 and the \"second protocol\" are unsourced, the gap claim is largely unsupported → UNSUPPORTED.\n\nI'll go PARTIAL to acknowledge the 20.56 part is real, with a note. Actually, let me reconsider: the two numbers 20.56 and 34.53. 20.56 is supported. 34.53 unsupported. The \"gap\" between two protocols — the \"two protocols\" framing itself is unsupported (only one protocol, Meta's, is in my sources). So really only 1/3 of the premise (the 20.56 number) is supported.\n\nHmm. I'll go with PARTIAL and note clearly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider test_summary vs question consistency. Both reference 20.56 and 34.53. I'll mark both PARTIAL.\n\nFor test_summary, note: \"The plan (what will be run) needs no source, but the reproduction target 20.56 matches GISMo's paper Hit@1 while 34.53 ('Rita et al.'s protocol') is unsourced and appears on no fetched page.\"\n\nFor question, note: \"The question premise cites no source; 20.56 matches GISMo's paper Hit@1, but 34.53 (Rita et al.'s protocol) is unsourced and appears on no fetched page.\"\n\nOK. Now let me also reconsider: for the \"SUPPORTED\" prediction items (if_lever, if_not), the page_quote field. Since there's no page, I'll write \"n/a — prediction requiring no source\" as page_quote. Actually, maybe I should leave a more meaningful string. The schema requires page_quote as string. I'll put \"n/a (prediction; no source required)\".\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe I should reconsider if_lever/if_not as UNSUPPORTED? No. They are predictions, explicitly exempt. SUPPORTED is fine. I'll clearly note \"prediction of the planned sweep; needs no source\".\n\nLet me now write the final JSON.\n\nFor page_quote fields, I'll use actual page text where applicable.\n\nLet me write out each entry.\n\nFor num items, include page_value as number.\n\nLet me finalize the exact page_quote strings:\n\n1. question: page_quote = \"GISMo (Ours) 31.51±0.10 20.56±0.14 35.89±0.16 54.27±0.19\"\n2. hypothesis: page_quote = \"GISMo (Ours) 31.51±0.10 20.56±0.14 35.89±0.16 54.27±0.19\"\n3. if_lever: page_quote = \"n/a (prediction; no source required)\"\n4. if_not: page_quote = \"n/a (prediction; no source required)\"\n5. test_summary: page_quote = \"GISMo (Ours) 31.51±0.10 20.56±0.14 35.89±0.16 54.27±0.19\"\n6. closest_prior: page_quote = \"We tuned the learning rate lr using the set {0.005,...}, and both the number of features F and ingredient embedding dimension d using the set {200,300,400,500,600}. ... The model uses F = 300 ... ingredient embedding dimension is set to d = 300.\"\n7. left_open: page_quote = \"and both the number of features F and ingredient embedding dimension d using the set {200,300,400,500,600}\"\n8. anchor: page_quote = \"GISMo (Ours) 31.51±0.10 20.56±0.14 35.89±0.16 54.27±0.19\"\n9. limits: page_quote = \"our benchmark contains a dataset of valid ingredient substitution pairs together with their corresponding recipes as well as standardized dataset splits and metrics.\"\n10. src:s1: page_quote = \"GISMo (Ours) 31.51±0.10 20.56±0.14 35.89±0.16 54.27±0.19\"\n11. src:s11: page_quote = \"Hits@1 Hits@3 | 20.4 35.6 | 20.3 35.9 | 20.6 36.2 | 20.6 36.1\"\n12. src:s6: page_quote = \"and both the number of features F and ingredient embedding dimension d using the set {200,300,400,500,600}\"\n13. num:vocab_size: page_quote = \"As a result, our ingredient vocabulary contains 6,653 ingredients.\" page_value 6653\n14. num:n_test: page_quote = \"we get 49,044, 10,729 and 10,747 substitution samples for training, validation, and test sets respectively.\" page_value 10747\n15. num:n_seeds: page_quote = \"Results are averaged over 5 runs, and the mean ± std are reported.\" page_value 5\n\nFor s11, the actual quote in the item is \"Hits@1 20.4 20.3 20.6 20.6\". The page (from search) shows the table as \"Hits@1 Hits@3 | 20.4 35.6 | 20.3 35.9 | 20.6 36.2 | 20.6 36.1\". So the \"Hits@1\" values are 20.4, 20.3, 20.6, 20.6. This matches. I'll use the page representation.\n\nNow let me write the final answer. I need to output ONLY the JSON, no other text.\n\nLet me write concise but clear notes.\n\nFor the \"page_quote\" of if_lever and if_not, I'll put a short placeholder.\n\nLet me write it all out.\n\nActually, for the notes, let me make them informative but concise.\n\nFinal JSON structure with all 15 items in the given order.\n\nLet me also double check the refs are exactly as given:\n- card:question\n- card:hypothesis\n- card:if_lever\n- card:if_not\n- card:test_summary\n- card:closest_prior\n- card:left_open\n- card:anchor\n- card:limits\n- src:s1\n- src:s11\n- src:s6\n- num:vocab_size\n- num:n_test\n- num:n_seeds\n\nYes.\n\nNow writing final JSON."
